/**
  @page guides-swift Fitting a mesh interface with SWIFT
  @brief Define an implicit circle, mark a mesh interface and fit it with a P1 displacement.

  This tutorial constructs a triangular mesh of the unit square, marks an
  internal polygonal interface and moves that interface towards a circle.
  A vector-valued @f$P_1@f$ displacement moves the vertices while retaining
  the mesh connectivity. The complete program is available in
  @ref Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp "InterfaceFitting.cpp".

  SWIFT fits selected mesh facets to the zero set of a supplied function.
  Its fitting force is distributed through the surrounding mesh, with
  deformation-quality checks during step acceptance. The
  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT "SWIFT namespace reference" explains the
  mathematical construction and solver parameters.

  @tableofcontents

  @section guides-swift-mesh 1. Create the background mesh

  The example uses local Eigen-backed storage. Its main headers and namespace
  aliases introduce the types used in the example.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Adaptation.h>
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>
  #include <Rodin/QF/QuadratureFormula.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>

  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;
  namespace SWIFT = Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT;
  @endcode

  A grid with 16 vertices per axis contains 450 triangles. Scaling by
  @f$h=1/15@f$ gives the physical domain @f$[0,1]^2@f$. Connectivity is
  prepared before cell classification and interface integration.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-mesh

  Cell attributes **Inside** and **Outside** distinguish the two regions.
  The facet attribute **Interface** selects the edges to fit. These labels
  identify entities of different dimensions.

  @section guides-swift-target 2. Define the target interface

  The target is the circle with center @f$c=(0.5,0.5)@f$ and radius
  @f$R=0.25@f$. Its implicit function and gradient are
  @f[
    \phi(x)=\lVert x-c\rVert-R,
    \qquad
    \nabla\phi(x)=\frac{x-c}{\lVert x-c\rVert}
    \quad (x\ne c).
  @f]
  Negative values identify the interior. Both functions are evaluated in
  physical coordinates, including displaced points during fitting.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-target

  The gradient has a zero fallback at the center, where radial distance is
  nondifferentiable. The target interface lies a positive distance from that
  point. Replacing the target requires a consistent gradient as well as a
  new implicit function.

  @section guides-swift-labels 3. Mark a starting mesh interface

  The initial interface must consist of existing mesh edges. MinSTCut
  classifies triangles using a cell average of a smoothed sign of
  @f$\phi@f$. Its cut edges separate the two resulting cell sets.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-classify

  The phase width and smoothing weight are choices for this background mesh.
  The quadrature loop computes the cell-average input; the following loops
  attach the returned labels to the mesh. Fitting then changes coordinates
  while preserving those labels and the connectivity.



  @section guides-swift-solve 4. Solve for a P1 displacement

  A two-component @f$P_1@f$ space represents the displacement
  @f$u_h:[0,1]^2\to\mathbb R^2@f$. Trial and test functions provide the
  variational interface to `SWIFT::Problem`.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-problem

  `model.h` is the reference mesh spacing. The example explicitly selects
  SparseLU for its small local system. `setInterfaceAttribute(Interface)`
  selects the marked internal edges, and `solve(phi, gradient)` stores the
  accumulated displacement in `u.getSolution()`.

  The outer square is free to move in this program. A fixed-boundary variant
  can add a vector `DirichletBC` on separately marked exterior facets; the
  @ref examples-swift-background "MMG–SWIFT tutorial" demonstrates that setup.
  The mesh, finite-element space and trial/test functions remain alive
  throughout the solve and displacement evaluation.

  @section guides-swift-check 5. Check the result before moving the mesh

  The solver report separates reaching the geometric target from satisfying
  the sampled deformation-quality budget. Both are required by this program.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-check

  `D_inf` is the maximum sampled gradient-normalized level-set residual. For
  this signed-distance target it measures radial error at the validation
  points. The automatic @f$P_1@f$ target is @f$h^2@f$. `min_j` and `max_Q`
  describe the sampled relative deformation Jacobian and distortion.
  The @ref swift-reference-results "report reference" defines these fields
  and the termination reasons.

  In the checked run, the sampled radial error was approximately
  @f$3.50389\times10^{-3}@f$, below @f$h^2=4.44444\times10^{-3}@f$.
  An independent calculation over complete fitted edge segments gave the
  same maximum radial error. All 450 moved triangles had positive signed
  Jacobians, and their connectivity was unchanged.

  @section guides-swift-move 6. Apply the displacement and export

  A separate mesh retains the fitted coordinates. Each vertex displacement
  is evaluated on the unchanged reference mesh.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-move

  For affine triangles and @f$P_1@f$ displacement, these vertex updates fully
  define the moved mesh geometry. The operation retains the 450 triangles
  and their incidence relations.

  @snippet Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp swift-p1-output

  `InterfaceFitting.xdmf` contains the reference **background** block with
  its displacement field and the fitted **moved** block. Keep the HDF5 files
  beside the XDMF description. In ParaView, select **Extract Block**, choose
  `moved`, enable **Surface With Edges** and color by the cell field
  `Attribute`.

  @m_class{rodin-mesh-stages}

  @parblock
  @image html SWIFTP1Tutorial-background.png "Initial P1 mesh. Cyan cells mark the classified interior; the navy circle is the target."
  @image html SWIFTP1Tutorial-moved.png "Computed P1 fit. Straight mesh edges approximate the circle; the navy curve shows the exact target."
  @endparblock

  @section guides-swift-run Build and run

  Use a configured local build with examples enabled. The
  @ref guides-getting-started-installation "installation guide" describes
  the dependencies. This example generates its mesh and needs no resource
  download or MMG step.

  @code{.sh}
  cmake --build build --target SWIFT_InterfaceFitting
  mkdir -p results/swift-p1
  cd results/swift-p1
  ../../build/examples/Adaptation/SWIFT/SWIFT_InterfaceFitting
  @endcode

  The executable returns failure when either the geometric target or the
  sampled quality budget is missed. The printed report and generated mesh
  provide the fit and geometry checks for a repeated run.

  @section guides-swift-curved Extension to P2

  SWIFT also supports @f$P_2@f$ displacement. The quadratic reconstruction
  example uses the same circle and background resolution, evaluates the
  solved field at every quadratic geometry node and retains the resulting
  curved transformations. The figure below shows that computed result.

  @image html SWIFTP2Tutorial-moved.png "Quadratic fit of the same circle. The checked run reached its geometric target with a sampled radial error of about 0.0000383 and satisfied the sampled quality budget." width=720px

  Higher-order geometry requires updating its edge and cell geometry nodes
  along with its vertices. The complete implementation is in
  @ref Adaptation/SWIFT/Reconstruction.cpp "Reconstruction.cpp"; the
  @ref Rodin::Adaptation::SWIFT "namespace reference" supplies the model,
  parameter definitions and sampling policies.

  @section guides-swift-source Complete source

  @ref Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp "InterfaceFitting.cpp" contains
  the complete P1 program. For background remeshing and fixed exterior
  vertices, continue with @ref examples-swift-background.

  @example Adaptation/SWIFT/InterfaceFitting.cpp
*/

/**
  @example Adaptation/SWIFT/Reconstruction.cpp
*/
